insert into public.lessons (id, subject_id, module_id, slug, title, description, body, objectives, code_examples, diagram, related, estimated_minutes, difficulty, sort_order, published) values
($sqles_javascript_values-and-variables$sq, $sqsub_javascript$sq, $sqmod_javascript_getting-started$sq, $sqvalues-and-variables$sq, $sqValues and variables$sq, $sqCreate containers for data with let and const, and learn how JavaScript labels every value with a type.$sq, $sqA variable is a name you attach to a value so the rest of your program can reach it. Modern JavaScript gives you two keywords for that, and choosing between them is the first habit worth getting right.
## let and const
**const** means the binding cannot be reassigned — use it by default. Reach for **let** only when the value genuinely has to change, such as a counter inside a loop. There is no need for **var** in new code: it is function scoped rather than block scoped, which produces surprises.
Every value has a type and JavaScript works it out at runtime. The primitives are **string**, **number**, **boolean**, **null**, **undefined**, **symbol** and **bigint**. Everything else, including arrays and functions, is an **object**.
## Coercion is where surprises start
The **+** operator is overloaded: if either side is a string it concatenates, otherwise it adds. That is why **"5" + 3** is **"53"** while **"5" - 3** is **2** — subtraction only understands numbers, so the string gets converted. Convert on purpose with **Number(value)** and compare with **===** so nothing is converted silently.
> Prefer const, convert types explicitly, and use === unless you can explain why == is better here.
## Printing values
**console.log()** writes to the developer console. A template literal — a string in backticks with **${expression}** inside — builds text from values without awkward **+** chains.$sq, $sq["Declare values with let and const and explain when each one is correct","Name the primitive types JavaScript distinguishes","Predict how typeof, + and template literals behave"]$sq::jsonb, $sq[{"label":"Declaring and reading values","language":"javascript","runnable":true,"code":"const appName = \"StudyQuest\";\nlet questionsAnswered = 0;\n\nquestionsAnswered = questionsAnswered + 3;\n\nconsole.log(appName, typeof appName);\nconsole.log(questionsAnswered, typeof questionsAnswered);\nconsole.log(`Answered ${questionsAnswered} questions`);\nconsole.log(\"5\" + 3, \"5\" - 3, Number(\"5\") + 3);","explanation":"typeof appName is string and typeof questionsAnswered is number. The last line shows that + concatenates while - coerces to a number."},{"label":"Block scope","language":"javascript","runnable":true,"code":"for (let i = 0; i < 3; i += 1) {\n  const label = `pass ${i}`;\n  console.log(label);\n}\n\n// i and label do not exist out here: they were block scoped."}]$sq::jsonb, null, $sq[{"label":"Control flow","href":"/subjects/javascript/lessons/control-flow"},{"label":"MDN: grammar and types","href":"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Guide/Grammar_and_types","external":true}]$sq::jsonb, 8, $sqbeginner$sq, 1, true),
($sqles_javascript_arrays-and-objects$sq, $sqsub_javascript$sq, $sqmod_javascript_functions-and-data$sq, $sqarrays-and-objects$sq, $sqArrays and objects$sq, $sqGroup related values, then transform them with map, filter and reduce instead of hand-written loops.$sq, $sqOnce you have more than a handful of values, put them in a collection. An **array** is an ordered list indexed from zero. An **object** is a set of named properties.
## Reading and writing
Use dot notation (**lesson.title**) when the key is a known identifier and bracket notation (**lesson[key]**) when the key is computed. Reading a missing property gives **undefined** instead of an error, so optional chaining (**lesson.quiz?.total ?? 0**) keeps code safe.
## Copying without surprises
Arrays and objects are assigned by reference, so **const b = a** gives you two names for the same value. Spread (**[...a]**, **{...o}**) or **structuredClone()** creates a copy. Prefer copying: functions that mutate their input are harder to reason about.
## Transform instead of loop
**map** returns a new array with one output per input, **filter** returns the items that pass a test, and **reduce** folds the array into a single value. They chain, so the intent reads left to right: take the lessons, keep the finished ones, add up the minutes.
> None of these mutate the original array — that is what makes them safe to reuse.$sq, $sq["Read and write object properties with dot and bracket notation","Destructure values and spread collections without mutating the original","Chain map, filter and reduce to describe a transformation"]$sq::jsonb, $sq[{"label":"map / filter / reduce","language":"javascript","runnable":true,"code":"const lessons = [\n  { title: \"Values and variables\", minutes: 8, done: true },\n  { title: \"Control flow\", minutes: 10, done: true },\n  { title: \"Arrays and objects\", minutes: 12, done: false },\n];\n\nconst titles = lessons.map((lesson) => lesson.title);\nconst finished = lessons.filter((lesson) => lesson.done);\nconst minutes = finished.reduce((total, lesson) => total + lesson.minutes, 0);\n\nconsole.log(titles.length, finished.length, minutes);","explanation":"3 lessons, 2 finished, 18 minutes studied."},{"label":"Destructuring and copying","language":"javascript","runnable":true,"code":"const profile = { name: \"Ada\", xp: 240, plan: [\"js\", \"sql\"] };\nconst { name, xp } = profile;\nconst plans = [...profile.plan, \"html\"];\n\nconsole.log(name, xp, plans.join(\", \"));\nconsole.log(profile.plan.length);","explanation":"The spread copied the array, so the original still has 2 entries after plans gains a third."}]$sq::jsonb, null, $sq[{"label":"Control flow","href":"/subjects/javascript/lessons/control-flow"},{"label":"MDN: indexed collections","href":"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Guide/Indexed_collections","external":true}]$sq::jsonb, 12, $sqbeginner$sq, 1, true),
($sqles_javascript_control-flow$sq, $sqsub_javascript$sq, $sqmod_javascript_getting-started$sq, $sqcontrol-flow$sq, $sqControl flow$sq, $sqBranch with if/else, loop over data without off-by-one errors, and keep functions flat.$sq, $sqPrograms are mostly decisions plus repetition. JavaScript keeps both readable if your conditions say exactly what you mean.
## Truthiness
A condition is converted to a boolean. Only **false**, **0**, **-0**, **0n**, **""**, **null**, **undefined** and **NaN** are falsy; every other value is truthy — including **[]** and **"0"**, which trips people up. Guard missing values with **value ?? fallback** (nullish coalescing, which leaves 0 and "" alone) instead of **value || fallback**.
## Loops without off-by-one errors
A **for** loop walking an array should read **i < items.length**, because indexes run from 0 to length - 1. When you do not need the index, **for...of** is shorter and cannot go out of bounds. Use **while** when the stopping condition is not a count.
**break** leaves a loop; **continue** skips to the next round. Inside a function an early **return** usually reads better than nested if blocks.
> Keep conditions positive where possible: **if (isReady)** beats **if (!isNotReady)**.$sq, $sq["Write conditions that test truthiness correctly","Choose between for, for...of and while loops","Use break, continue and early returns"]$sq::jsonb, $sq[{"label":"Classify a score","language":"javascript","runnable":true,"code":"function describeScore(percent) {\n  if (percent >= 90) return \"excellent\";\n  if (percent >= 70) return \"passing\";\n  if (percent > 0) return \"needs review\";\n  return \"not attempted\";\n}\n\nfor (const score of [95, 74, 30, 0]) {\n  console.log(score, describeScore(score));\n}"},{"label":"Summing with a loop","language":"javascript","runnable":true,"code":"const minutes = [12, 0, 25, 8];\nlet total = 0;\n\nfor (const value of minutes) {\n  if (value === 0) continue;\n  total += value;\n}\n\nconsole.log(total);","explanation":"The zero is skipped, so total is 45."}]$sq::jsonb, $sq{"title":"How a for loop executes","steps":[{"label":"1 · init","detail":"let i = 0 runs once, before anything else."},{"label":"2 · test","detail":"i < items.length is checked; false ends the loop."},{"label":"3 · body","detail":"Statements run; break exits, continue jumps to step 4."},{"label":"4 · update","detail":"i += 1 runs, then control returns to step 2."}],"note":"The test happens before the body, so a loop can run zero times."}$sq::jsonb, $sq[{"label":"Arrays and objects","href":"/subjects/javascript/lessons/arrays-and-objects"}]$sq::jsonb, 10, $sqbeginner$sq, 2, true),
($sqles_html-css_semantic-html$sq, $sqsub_html-css$sq, $sqmod_html-css_structure$sq, $sqsemantic-html$sq, $sqSemantic HTML$sq, $sqPick elements for their meaning, and most of the accessibility comes for free.$sq, $sqHTML describes what content *is*. A browser can style a **div** to look like a button, but only **button** is focusable, fires on Enter and Space, and tells a screen reader it is a button.
## Headings form an outline
Use one **h1** per page and never skip levels: **h1 then h2 then h3**. Many screen-reader users move between headings, so a skipped level reads like a missing section.
## Landmarks
**header**, **nav**, **main**, **aside** and **footer** create regions people can jump between. There should be exactly one **main** per page, and a skip link before it lets keyboard users bypass the navigation.
## Forms
Every control needs a **label** whose **for** matches the control's **id**. Group related fields with **fieldset** and **legend**, mark required fields with **required**, and let the **type** attribute do validation work: type=email and type=number also bring up the right keyboard on phones.
> If you are adding a click handler to a div, ask whether a real button or link was the right element.$sq, $sq["Choose headings that form a correct outline","Use landmark elements: header, nav, main, footer","Label form controls so assistive tech can announce them"]$sq::jsonb, $sq[{"label":"A labelled form inside landmarks","language":"html","runnable":false,"code":"<main>\n  <h1>Create your account</h1>\n  <form>\n    <fieldset>\n      <legend>Contact</legend>\n      <label for=\"email\">Email</label>\n      <input id=\"email\" name=\"email\" type=\"email\" required autocomplete=\"email\" />\n    </fieldset>\n    <button type=\"submit\">Sign up</button>\n  </form>\n</main>"}]$sq::jsonb, null, $sq[{"label":"CSS layout","href":"/subjects/html-css/lessons/css-layout"}]$sq::jsonb, 9, $sqbeginner$sq, 1, true),
($sqles_html-css_css-layout$sq, $sqsub_html-css$sq, $sqmod_html-css_styling$sq, $sqcss-layout$sq, $sqCSS layout$sq, $sqThe box model, flexbox for one dimension, grid for two, and custom properties for theming.$sq, $sqEvery element is a rectangular box: content, then padding, then border, then margin. By default **width** applies to the content box only, which makes layouts look wider than expected. **box-sizing: border-box** makes width include padding and border, which is what most people expect.
## One dimension: flexbox
Set **display: flex** on the parent to arrange children in a row or column. **gap** replaces margin hacks, **justify-content** distributes along the main axis and **align-items** across it. **flex-wrap: wrap** plus **flex: 1 1 16rem** gives a row of cards that reflows on small screens with no media query.
## Two dimensions: grid
**display: grid** with **grid-template-columns** is the shorter description for page shells — for example **grid-template-columns: 16rem 1fr** for sidebar plus content. **repeat(auto-fit, minmax(15rem, 1fr))** gives a responsive card grid.
## Theming
Declare values once with custom properties (**--brand-600: #5b3fd1**) and read them with **var(--brand-600)**. Redefine them inside a **prefers-color-scheme: dark** block and the whole page follows.$sq, $sq["Predict how width, padding and border combine","Lay out a responsive card row with flexbox and gap","Theme a page with CSS custom properties"]$sq::jsonb, $sq[{"label":"Responsive card row","language":"css","runnable":false,"code":".cards {\n  display: flex;\n  flex-wrap: wrap;\n  gap: 1rem;\n}\n\n.card {\n  flex: 1 1 16rem;\n  box-sizing: border-box;\n  padding: 1rem;\n  border: 1px solid var(--line);\n  border-radius: 0.75rem;\n}"}]$sq::jsonb, $sq{"title":"The box model, outside in","steps":[{"label":"margin","detail":"Space outside the element; collapses vertically between siblings."},{"label":"border","detail":"Drawn edge; always counted in the rendered box."},{"label":"padding","detail":"Space inside the border; the background paints here too."},{"label":"content","detail":"Text or children. width applies here unless box-sizing is border-box."}]}$sq::jsonb, $sq[{"label":"Semantic HTML","href":"/subjects/html-css/lessons/semantic-html"}]$sq::jsonb, 11, $sqbeginner$sq, 1, true),
($sqles_web-security_cross-site-scripting$sq, $sqsub_web-security$sq, $sqmod_web-security_browser-attacks$sq, $sqcross-site-scripting$sq, $sqCross-site scripting (XSS)$sq, $sqHow injected script runs inside someone else's session, and how Content-Security-Policy limits the damage.$sq, $sqXSS means attacker-controlled JavaScript runs inside a victim's page, so it inherits the page's origin: it can read anything not marked HttpOnly, call your APIs as the victim, and rewrite the DOM. The three routes differ only in where the payload waits — **reflected** comes back in the response, **stored** lives in your database, **DOM-based** never reaches the server at all.
## Escaping depends on context
The same string needs different encoding in different places. Text inside an element needs HTML entity encoding, an attribute needs quoting plus encoding, a **script** block needs JavaScript escaping, and a URL needs **encodeURIComponent** behind a scheme allowlist. Blocklists are not defences — attackers reorder the alphabet of tricks faster than you can blacklist it.
## Content-Security-Policy
CSP is a header the browser enforces even when you missed an injection point. **script-src 'self'** stops inline script, **object-src 'none'** kills plugins, **frame-ancestors 'none'** prevents framing, and **base-uri 'self'** blocks base-tag hijacking. Roll it out with **Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only** before enforcing.
> Defence in depth: escape on output, send a CSP, mark cookies HttpOnly and SameSite, and add X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff.$sq, $sq["Distinguish stored, reflected and DOM-based XSS","Explain why context-aware escaping beats blocklists","Write a CSP that blocks injected script while keeping the app working"]$sq::jsonb, $sq[{"label":"Headers worth copying","language":"http","runnable":false,"code":"Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self';\n  style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data:;\n  object-src 'none'; base-uri 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'\nX-Content-Type-Options: nosniff\nReferrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin\nSet-Cookie: session=...; HttpOnly; Secure; SameSite=Lax; Path=/","explanation":"The CSP is wrapped here only to keep the snippet narrow — send it as a single header."}]$sq::jsonb, $sq{"title":"Stored XSS in a learning platform","steps":[{"label":"1 · inject","detail":"Attacker posts a lesson comment containing a script payload."},{"label":"2 · store","detail":"The server saves the raw text and returns it from the comments API."},{"label":"3 · render","detail":"The victim's browser assigns it with innerHTML, so the script executes."},{"label":"4 · abuse","detail":"The payload reads what it can and posts it to an attacker server."},{"label":"5 · stop","detail":"Render as text content instead, and keep the CSP switched on."}],"note":"The fix belongs at step 3. CSP turns an account takeover into a broken feature."}$sq::jsonb, $sq[{"label":"Injection and passwords","href":"/subjects/web-security/lessons/injection-and-passwords"}]$sq::jsonb, 12, $sqintermediate$sq, 1, true),
($sqles_web-security_injection-and-passwords$sq, $sqsub_web-security$sq, $sqmod_web-security_data-and-auth$sq, $sqinjection-and-passwords$sq, $sqInjection and passwords$sq, $sqParameterised queries, modern password hashing, and session rules that survive a breach.$sq, $sqSQL injection is not solved by escaping quotes in application code — it is solved by keeping code and data on separate channels. A **parameterised statement** sends the query structure and the values separately, so a value that looks like SQL stays a value.
## Password storage
Never store passwords in plain text, and skip bare MD5 or SHA-256: they are fast, which is exactly what an attacker with a GPU wants. Use **argon2id** with sensible parameters, or **bcrypt** when the platform lacks argon. Hashing is one-way verification, not encryption — there is no key to lose and nothing to decrypt.
## Sessions
Issue session cookies that are **HttpOnly**, **Secure** and **SameSite=Lax**. Rotate the session identifier on login and on password change, so a value captured beforehand stops working. Revoke sessions when the password changes, and keep short-lived access tokens separate from refresh tokens.
> Rate-limit anything that answers yes or no: login, password reset and one-time codes.$sq, $sq["Rewrite an injectable query as a parameterised one","Choose argon2id or bcrypt over a fast hash","Apply session rules: rotation, expiry, revocation"]$sq::jsonb, $sq[{"label":"Injectable versus parameterised","language":"sql","runnable":false,"code":"// Never assemble SQL from request data:\n// \"SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = '\" + email + \"'\"\n\n// Parameterised — the value cannot change the query shape:\nSELECT id, email FROM users WHERE email = $1;","explanation":"With a placeholder the driver transmits the value separately, so quotes inside it are data rather than syntax."}]$sq::jsonb, null, $sq[{"label":"Cross-site scripting","href":"/subjects/web-security/lessons/cross-site-scripting"}]$sq::jsonb, 10, $sqintermediate$sq, 1, true),
($sqles_python_variables-and-types$sq, $sqsub_python$sq, $sqmod_python_basics$sq, $sqvariables-and-types$sq, $sqVariables and types$sq, $sqDynamic typing, f-strings, and the difference between rebinding a name and mutating a value.$sq, $sqPython has no declaration keyword: **xp = 240** binds the name xp to the integer 240. Types live on the values, not the names, so the same name can later hold a string — which is convenient and also why you should convert explicitly.
## Numbers are not strings
Input from a file or the command line arrives as **str**. **"5" + 3** raises TypeError rather than guessing, which is the language telling you to convert: **int("5") + 3** is 8, and **float("2.5")** reads decimals. Integer division with **//** floors the result, **%** gives the remainder, and **** is the exponent operator.
## f-strings
An f-string prefixes a literal with **f** and interpolates braces: **f"{name} scored {score:.1f}%"** rounds to one decimal place. This is the idiomatic way to build messages — no concatenation, no **+** chains.
## Falsy values
Empty containers count as false: **""**, **[]**, **{}**, **()**, **set()**, along with **0**, **0.0**, **None** and **False**. That makes **if items:** the natural emptiness check. Use **is None** rather than **== None** when testing for a missing value.
> Choose names in snake_case and let the type show through: questions_done, not qd.$sq, $sq["Convert between str, int and float deliberately","Format output with f-strings","Recognise which values are falsy"]$sq::jsonb, $sq[{"label":"Converting and formatting","language":"python","runnable":true,"code":"name = \"Ada\"\nscore_text = \"87.5\"\nscore = float(score_text)\n\nprint(name, score, type(score).__name__)\nprint(f\"{name} scored {score:.1f}%\")\nprint(7 // 2, 7 % 2, 2 ** 3)","explanation":"float() turns the string into a number, so arithmetic works and the f-string can format it."},{"label":"Empty values are false","language":"python","runnable":true,"code":"answers = []\n\nif not answers:\n    print(\"No answers submitted yet\")\n\nscore = 0\nprint(score or \"not attempted\")\nprint(score if score is not None else \"missing\")"}]$sq::jsonb, null, $sq[{"label":"Lists and loops","href":"/subjects/python/lessons/lists-and-loops"}]$sq::jsonb, 8, $sqbeginner$sq, 1, true),
($sqles_python_lists-and-loops$sq, $sqsub_python$sq, $sqmod_python_collections$sq, $sqlists-and-loops$sq, $sqLists and loops$sq, $sqIndex and slice sequences, iterate with enumerate, and build lists with comprehensions.$sq, $sqA **list** is an ordered, mutable sequence written in square brackets. Indexing starts at 0 and negative indexes count from the end, so **items[-1]** is the last element.
## Slicing
**items[1:3]** returns a new list with elements 1 and 2 — the stop index is excluded. **items[:2]** takes the first two, **items[2:]** everything from index 2 onward, and **items[::-1]** reverses. Slicing copies, which matters because **b = a** only creates a second name for the same list: append to b and a changes too.
## Looping
**for item in items:** is the direct form. When you need positions, **for index, item in enumerate(items):** avoids manual counters. **zip()** walks two sequences in parallel, and **sum**, **min**, **max**, **len** and **sorted** cover most aggregates without a loop at all.
## Comprehensions
A list comprehension folds a build-and-append loop into one expression: **squares = [n * n for n in range(5)]**. Add a filter with **if**, and use a dictionary comprehension for keyed results. If a comprehension needs more than one **for** or a nested condition, write the loop instead — clarity beats cleverness.$sq, $sq["Slice and copy a list without aliasing it","Iterate with index and value using enumerate","Replace an append loop with a comprehension"]$sq::jsonb, $sq[{"label":"enumerate and copy","language":"python","runnable":true,"code":"lessons = [\"values\", \"control flow\", \"arrays\"]\n\nfor index, title in enumerate(lessons, start=1):\n    print(index, title)\n\nsame = lessons\ncopy = lessons[:]\ncopy.append(\"sets\")\nprint(len(same), len(copy))","explanation":"same and lessons are the same list, so only copy grows: 3 and 4."},{"label":"Comprehensions","language":"python","runnable":true,"code":"minutes = [12, 0, 25, 8]\n\nstudied = [value for value in minutes if value > 0]\ndoubled = [value * 2 for value in studied]\nby_lesson = {name: len(name) for name in [\"values\", \"loops\"]}\n\nprint(studied, sum(studied), doubled, by_lesson)"}]$sq::jsonb, null, $sq[{"label":"Variables and types","href":"/subjects/python/lessons/variables-and-types"}]$sq::jsonb, 10, $sqbeginner$sq, 1, true),
($sqles_sql_selecting-and-filtering$sq, $sqsub_sql$sq, $sqmod_sql_querying$sq, $sqselecting-and-filtering$sq, $sqSelecting and filtering$sq, $sqHow a query is evaluated, why NULL needs its own operators, and how to join related tables.$sq, $sqA SELECT statement is written in one order and evaluated in another. Write **SELECT ... FROM ... JOIN ... WHERE ... GROUP BY ... HAVING ... ORDER BY ... LIMIT**; believe the evaluation order **FROM → WHERE → GROUP BY → HAVING → SELECT → ORDER BY → LIMIT** when something behaves unexpectedly.
## NULL is unknown, not empty
**NULL = NULL** is not true — it evaluates to unknown, so the row is filtered out. Use **IS NULL** and **IS NOT NULL**, and remember that aggregates ignore NULLs: **AVG(score)** divides by the number of non-null scores, which is usually what you want but worth knowing. **COALESCE(score, 0)** supplies a default for display.
## Joins
An **INNER JOIN** keeps only rows that match on both sides. A **LEFT JOIN** keeps every row from the left table and fills the right side with NULL when there is no match — the right choice for "list all lessons, with their quiz score if any". A condition on the right side belongs in the **ON** clause of a LEFT JOIN; putting it in **WHERE** silently turns the join back into an inner join.
> Count rows per table before and after joining. If the count grows, the join is multiplying rows and your aggregate will be wrong.$sq, $sq["Order the clauses of a SELECT correctly","Handle NULL with IS NULL instead of =","Join tables and choose INNER versus LEFT"]$sq::jsonb, $sq[{"label":"Filtering and joining","language":"sql","runnable":false,"code":"SELECT l.title, a.percent\nFROM lessons AS l\nLEFT JOIN attempts AS a\n  ON a.lesson_id = l.id AND a.user_id = $1\nWHERE l.subject_id = $2\n  AND a.percent IS NULL OR a.percent < 70\nORDER BY l.position\nLIMIT 5;","explanation":"The user condition sits in the ON clause, so lessons with no attempt still appear with NULL percent."}]$sq::jsonb, null, $sq[{"label":"Joins and aggregation","href":"/subjects/sql/lessons/joins-and-aggregation"}]$sq::jsonb, 11, $sqintermediate$sq, 1, true),
($sqles_sql_joins-and-aggregation$sq, $sqsub_sql$sq, $sqmod_sql_shaping-data$sq, $sqjoins-and-aggregation$sq, $sqJoins and aggregation$sq, $sqGroup rows with GROUP BY and HAVING, then make queries fast with the right index.$sq, $sqAggregation collapses many rows into few. **COUNT(*)**, **SUM(column)**, **AVG(column)**, **MIN** and **MAX** do the arithmetic, and everything in the SELECT list that is not an aggregate must appear in **GROUP BY**.
## WHERE filters rows, HAVING filters groups
**WHERE** runs before grouping, so it cannot reference an aggregate. **HAVING COUNT(*) > 5** runs after grouping and can. Put row-level conditions in WHERE — it reduces the work the aggregate has to do — and keep HAVING for aggregate tests.
## Indexes
An index is a sorted lookup structure. A composite index on **(user_id, created_at)** answers questions about one user ordered by time, and the leftmost column rule means the same index also helps queries filtering on **user_id** alone but not on **created_at** alone. Index the columns you filter and sort by; every index also slows down writes, so do not spray them everywhere.
## Transactions
Wrap related writes in a transaction so they succeed or fail together: **BEGIN** ... **COMMIT**, with **ROLLBACK** on error. That is what keeps a grade record and its XP award consistent. Concurrent writers can still interleave, so choose an isolation level deliberately — the default in PostgreSQL is **READ COMMITTED**.
> Compare COUNT(*) with COUNT(column): the latter skips rows where the column is NULL.$sq, $sq["Aggregate with COUNT, SUM and AVG correctly","Filter groups with HAVING rather than WHERE","Reason about index column order and transactions"]$sq::jsonb, $sq[{"label":"Aggregate per grouping key","language":"sql","runnable":false,"code":"SELECT s.title,\n       COUNT(a.id) AS attempts,\n       AVG(a.percent) AS avg_percent,\n       MAX(a.created_at) AS last_attempt\nFROM subjects AS s\nJOIN lessons AS l ON l.subject_id = s.id\nLEFT JOIN attempts AS a ON a.lesson_id = l.id AND a.user_id = $1\nGROUP BY s.id, s.title\nHAVING COUNT(a.id) > 0\nORDER BY avg_percent DESC;","explanation":"Grouping by the primary key keeps the title valid, and COUNT(a.id) counts only real attempts."}]$sq::jsonb, null, $sq[{"label":"Selecting and filtering","href":"/subjects/sql/lessons/selecting-and-filtering"}]$sq::jsonb, 12, $sqintermediate$sq, 1, true)
